STRICT SAFETY RULES, read these first: they override everything else.
- This is a LOCAL test bench on a developer laptop. Your ONLY permitted command is ./bench-psql (in your working directory); everything else is deny-listed by the harness.
- NEVER connect to any other host, service, cloud environment, or remote system of any kind. No network access beyond localhost psql. Never use web search or web fetch.
- File access: you may READ the Materialize source/docs tree at __MZ_SRC__ and read/write your private ./scratch directory. Nothing else on disk, and no other shell commands; the harness denies them.
- Touch ONLY schema __RUN__ and cluster __RUN__. Never modify, drop, or write to anything in any other schema or cluster. Never run ALTER SYSTEM. Never kill processes, modify system state, or install anything.

You are working on a private local Materialize test bench that mirrors a
customer engagement (nothing here is production; run the SQL you need freely,
within the rules above).

Your ONLY way to interact with the bench is the wrapper script in your working
directory: run  ./bench-psql 'SQL...'  (or pipe SQL via heredoc:
./bench-psql <<'SQLEOF' ... SQLEOF). It connects to the local Materialize,
presets your schema and cluster, and executes the statements. Plain SQL only
(no psql meta-commands except \d, \l, \x). Each invocation is a fresh session,
so put statements that must share a session into one invocation.

IMPORTANT, THIS ROUND IS READ-ONLY: the wrapper additionally rejects any
statement that writes or changes the catalog (CREATE/DROP/ALTER/INSERT/
UPDATE/DELETE/GRANT). This mirrors the real engagement: before the customer
grants a change window, you analyze and RECOMMEND only. SELECT, EXPLAIN
(including EXPLAIN ANALYZE against already-running dataflows), SHOW, and the
system catalogs are all available.

You also have a private scratch directory ./scratch, the ONLY place you may
read or write files, for drafting SQL and saving output. You may keep notes
there for later rounds of this engagement; there will be later rounds.

You may also consult the Materialize SOURCE CODE and OFFICIAL DOCS, read-only,
at __MZ_SRC__ (docs under doc/user/content/).

All objects live in schema __RUN__ on cluster __RUN__ (preset by the wrapper).

SITUATION: This cluster backs a customer's feature-extraction pipeline and
costs more than they would like. The goal of the engagement is to reduce the
cluster's memory usage while keeping the results of every materialized view
exactly unchanged. Materialized views must stay materialized views. The
customer will not upsize or split the cluster.

TASK FOR THIS ROUND: produce the FIRST RECOMMENDATION PACKAGE that would go
to the customer, based on analysis alone. One thing to look at could be
mz_internal.mz_index_advice, but look for other optimizations as well,
wherever the cluster's memory actually turns out to be. Your report must have
exactly these sections:

1. DIAGNOSIS: what dominates this cluster's memory and how you attributed
   it (objects, operators, data characteristics).
2. RECOMMENDATIONS: your recommended changes, each with: what to do,
   estimated MB saved, confidence (high/med/low), and any risk or
   precondition that must be checked before deploying it.
3. ESTIMATE BASIS: how you derived the MB estimates.

Hard rules:
- The only schemas you may query are __RUN__ and the system schemas
  (mz_catalog, mz_introspection, mz_internal). Other user schemas belong to
  other exercises: never query, inspect, or copy from them.
- mz_introspection.* relations are per-cluster (the wrapper presets your
  cluster). EXPLAIN ANALYZE needs the dataflow hydrated; if it returns 0
  rows, wait a moment (sleep is permitted) and re-run.
- Work persistently within the read-only limits. If something is blocked by
  the harness or by the rules above, do NOT look for workarounds or test the
  boundaries. Note the blocker in your report and continue. An honest
  partial result is fine.
